"""Explain how macros are recursively expanded in c"""," ""Macros in C are expanded during compilation, not at runtime. When the compiler encounters a macro invocation, it替换s the macro with its definition. This process can be recursive, meaning a macro might expand to other macros, which in turn expand, potentially creating a chain of replacements. For example, consider a macro that defines a function: #define MyFunc() { printf(""Hello, world!\\n""); } Using this macro in a program: MyFunc(); The compiler would replace MyFunc() with { printf(""Hello, world!\\n""); }, resulting in the output Hello, world! MacDonald's brace expansion further simplifies this process by automatically generating proper curly braces around the macro's body."""
