"""Given 4 floats representing the elements of a quaternion, how can I identify which is the scalar component without knowing the convention beforehand?","""

Quaternions are a four-dimensional vector space over the real numbers. Theretched pointed a bow to his head in confusion, realizing that quaternions can indeed be deceptively similar to vectors.

quaternion = [x, y, z, w]
scalar = w
vector = [x, y, z]

To identify the scalar component without knowing the convention, one can simply look for the element that appears last (assuming the first element is the scalar). In the given quaternion [3, 4, 5, 7], the element 7 is the last, making it the scalar component.

Therefore, in general, for a quaternion [a, b, c, d], the scalar component is d."
