rman, 2017, Soulard, 2017). Also, effects on ecosystems functioning were highlighted by several authors (Li et al., 2005, Lehv\\u00e4virta et al., 2006, Mingyu et al., 2009, Pickering et al., 2010, Ciach et al., 2017, Pablo-Cea et al., 2020).\\n\\nAnyway, hiking trails tend to be narrower and have less infrastructures associated than asphalt, concrete or cobblestone paved roads, or even unpaved gravel roads (Ballantyne and Pickering, 2015b, Pickering and Norman, 2017, Swart et al., 2019). Their characteristics, both in terms of width and surface on which users tread, tend to have impacts that are probably lesser in intensity and reach than that of the aforementioned types of roads (Wolf and Croft, 2014, Pickering and Norman, 2017, Swart et al., 2019), with the exception of soil loss (Ballantyne and Pickering 2015b). Nonetheless, some exceptions exist: unlike a hiking trail, that normally would be too narrow for four-wheeled motorized vehicles, dirt roads are more prone to a mixed usage (Pickering et al., 2010, Wolf and Croft, 2014, Havlick et al., 2016). In this way, even if dirt roads are wider than a normal path and, consequentially, affect a greater area than their narrower counterparts (Wolf and Croft, 2014, Ballantyne and Pickering, 2015b), it is harder to disentangle the effects of each type of use they are subjected to (Pickering et al., 2010, Wolf and Croft, 2014, Ballantyne and Pickering, 2015b). Another example would be the use of motorcycles, as these should be able to still venture into most paths that would normally be used for hiking, aggravating possible impacts of walking or running (Wilson and Seney, 1994, Pickering et al., 2010, Havlick et al., 2016). Following the same logic, the use of bicycles and horses also represents an additional type of use that may exacerbate the impacts of a typical hiking trail (T\\u00f6rn et al., 2009, Pickering et al., 2010, Havlick et al., 2016, Svajda et al., 2016). Despite the potential confounding facto"",""\\u041f\\u0440\\u043e\\u0434\\u043e\\u043b\\u0436\\u0438, \\u043f\\u043e\\u0436\\u0430\\u043b\\u0443\\u0439\\u0441\\u0442\\u0430"",""\\u0410 \\u0442\\u0435\\u043f\\u0435\\u0440\\u044c \\u0441\\u0444\\u043e\\u0440\\u043c\\u0443\\u043b\\u0438\\u0440\\u0443\\u0439 \\u044d\\u0442\\u043e \\u0432 \\u0432\\u0438\\u0434\\u0435 \\u043d\\u0435\\u0431\\u043e\\u043b\\u044c\\u0448\\u043e\\u0439 \\u0437\\u0430\\u043c\\u0435\\u0442\\u043a\\u0438 (20 \\u0441\\u0442\\u0440\\u043e\\u0447\\u0435\\u043a), \\u043f\\u043e\\u0436\\u0430\\u043b\\u0443\\u0439\\u0441\\u0442\\u0430. \\u0421\\u043e \\u0441\\u043c\\u0430\\u0439\\u043b\\u0438\\u043a\\u0430\\u043c\\u0438""",ERROR: 400
