The image depicts cross-sections of Lowry diamonds, showing their internal structure and hexagonal crystal lattices. Lowry diamonds are named after Sir Nevil Lowry, a British mineralogist who first characterized them in the early 20th century.

These cross-sections allow us to see the distinctive hexagonal prismatic structure of Lowry diamonds, which is crucial for understanding their crystallographic properties. The internal arrangement of these hexagons gives Lowry diamonds their unique optical and physical characteristics.

Lowry diamonds are important in the field of mineralogy and materials science, as they provide valuable insights into crystal growth processes and defect structures in diamond-like materials. The ability to observe their cross-sections offers researchers and enthusiasts a detailed look at the beautiful and complex internal arrangement of this fascinating mineral."
