"""Evaluate \\\\displaystyle \\\\iint\\\\limits_{D}{{2y{x^2} + 9{y^3}\\\\,dA}} where D is the region bounded by \\\\displaystyle y = \\\\frac{2}{3}x and y = 2\\\\sqrt x."""," Evaluate the double integral

\\\\[
\\\\iint\\\\limits_{D}{{2y{x^2} + 9{y^3}\\\\,dA}}
\\]
where D is the region bounded by
\\[ y = \\frac{2}{3}x \\]
and
\\[ y = 2\\sqrt x. \\]

To solve this double integral, we need to convert it to polar coordinates. The region D can be described in polar coordinates as follows:

\\[ 0 \\leq r \\leq 3 \\]
\\[ \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]

The Jacobian for the polar transformation is r, so the integral becomes:

\\[ \\int\\limits_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\int\\limits_{0}^3 \\left( 2 \\left( r \\sin \\theta \\right)^2 \\left( r \\cos^2 \\theta \\right) + 9 \\left( r \\sin \\theta \\right)^3 \\right) r \\, dr \\, dθ \\]

Simplifying:

\\[ \\int\\limits_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\int\\limits_{0}^3 \\left( 2r^5 \\sin^2 \\theta \\cos^2 \\theta + 9r^5 \\sin^3 \\theta \\right) \\, dr \\, dθ \\]

Using the power reduction formulas:
\\[ \\sin^2 \\theta \\cos^2 \\theta = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin^2(2\\theta) \\]
\\[ \\sin^3 \\theta = \\frac{3}{4} \\sin \\theta \\left( 1 - \\cos^2 \\theta \\right) \\]

\\[ \\int\\limits_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\int\\limits_0^3 r^6 \\left( \\frac{1}{4} \\sin^2(2\\theta) + \\frac{9}{4} \\sin \\theta \\left( 1 - \\cos^2 \\theta \\right) \\right) \\, dr \\, dθ \\]

Evaluating these integrals:

\\[ \\frac{1}{7} \\left( \\frac{3^7}{2} \\frac{1}{27} + \\frac{5}{81} 3^5 + \\frac{1}{6} 3^3 - \\frac{9}{20} 3^5 - \\frac{1}{12} 3^3```\\)

