"""Why are trans fats bad?"," Why? What types of foods contain trans fats?""

These are common questions people ask about trans fats. Trans fats are man-made fats created through a partial hydrogenation process in food manufacturing. They were once thought to be beneficial, but we now understand they are harmful to our health.

Trans fatty acids consist of two saturated carbon atoms in the middle of their carbon chain, while all the hydrogen atoms are on the outer carbon atoms. This unique structure sets them apart from other saturated fats and makes them potentially dangerous when consumed in large amounts.

Trans fats are primarily found in industrially produced, hydrogenated foods such as:

- Margarine and spreadable butters
- Pre-made snacks like chips and cookies
- Fried foods prepared in partially hydrogenated vegetable oils
- Processed baked goods like donuts and crackers

It's important to note that natural fats found in whole foods like avocados, nuts, and certain types of fish are not considered trans fats. The health community strongly encourages limitations on trans fat intake to reduce the risk of heart disease and other health problems."
