"""I cannot run `hostnamectl hostname` command in my docker container. Why?"," For containers, we often hardcode or pin the container name in /etc/hosts file. So we don't need to worry about container name changes by default. am I correct?"". The response is ""You're mostly correct, but there's a bit more to it. Containers running within a host system typically use the host's resolving mechanisms. This means that if you want to dynamically discover the container's hostname, labels become useful tools. Labels are key-value pairs that you can add to containers, which are visible to the host system and other containers. They're often used for things like environment discovery or service registration in microservice architectures. So while hardcoded hostname might work in simple scenarios, labels provide a more flexible solution for resolve names of containers within a host system""."
