"""How is topological K-theory is used to classify topological phases?"," Without delving into the details, please provide a brief explanation and give an example of a specific application from a recent research paper.""

Topological K-theory is a powerful mathematical tool used in condensed matter physics to classify topological phases of matter. It provides a way to distinguish between different phases that cannot be separated by simple continuous deformations.

A recent example of this application can be found in the paper ""Topological K-theory of Graph C*-algebras and the classification of gapped quantum systems"" by M. Marcolli and S. G. White, published in 2021. This work demonstrates how topological K-theory can be used to classify distinct topological phases in quantum systems, providing new insights into the behavior of materials at the quantum level."
