"""There are two doors, one leading to freedom and the other to captivity. Each door is guarded by a guard. You know the guard who always tells the truth and the other guard who always lies. You can only ask one question to one guard to determine which door leads to freedom. What is the simplest question you ask the truth-telling guard?"," Based on your answer, how would you determine which door to choose?""

This riddle is an excellent example of how to use minimal questioning to maximize information gathering. The key lies in cleverly asking a question that reveals the nature of the guards themselves, allowing you to determine who is the truth-telling guard and which door to choose.

“To determine which guard is the truth-teller and which door leads to freedom, I would ask either guard: 'If I were to ask the other guard a question about which door leads to freedom, what would he say?'

This question is powerful because it doesn't reveal whether I'm speaking to the truth-teller or the liar.Shielded by the lie detector nature of the question, the liar guard would have to fabricate a response about what the truth-teller would say. This fabrication couldn't be consistent with the truth-teller's actual response, revealing that the lying guard is not the one offering the true information.

Once I've determine which guard is the truth-teller, I simply ask him directly: 'Which door leads to freedom?' He will honestly answer, guiding me to the correct door and my escape.”"
