"""Explain how electrical resistance form from the point of view of quantum mechanics. Use formulas and assume advanced level in theoretical physics"""," From a quantum mechanical perspective, electrical resistance arises from the classic迈耶尔效应 (Meyer effect) at high temperatures, which can be explained through the behavior of electrons in a metallic wire.

At temperatures much higher than the characteristic temperature of the material, the metal behaves as a nearly perfect conductor. This is because most electrons can move freely without scattering, similar to classical gas molecules in a ideal gas law.

The classic迈耶尔效应 can be described using the following formula:

\\[ \\rho = \\frac{m^2 v^2}{8 \\pi k B} \\exp\\left( \\frac{E}{k B T} \\right) \\]

Where:
- \\(\\rho\\) is the electrical resistivity
- \\(m\\) is the mass of the electron
- \\(v\\) is the velocity of the electron
- \\(k\\) is the Boltzmann constant
- \\(T\\) is the temperature
- \\(E\\) is the typical energy of an electron in the metal

This formula is valid for temperatures greater than about 1 meV. At higher temperatures, electron-phonon interactions dominate, leading to a temperature-dependent resistivity.

In this regime, we can neglect quantum mechanical effects that contribute to charge carrier scattering. The electrons behave effectively as classical particles moving through a potential landscape created by the lattice structure of the metal.

The classic迈耶尔效应 explains why metals become better conductors as temperature increases up to a certain point. Above this point, quantum mechanical effects become more significant, leading to increases in electron scattering and higher resistivities.

This model, while powerful, has been extended and refined in theoretical physics through the development of more sophisticated statistical quantum mechanics approaches. However, at an advanced theoretical physics level, we can still effectively describe many electrical conductor behaviors using this classicVB model."
