"""A pump can fill a pool in 10 hrs. Another pump can fill it in 15 hrs. How long will it take if both pumps work together?"," We can solve this as 1/10 + 1/15 = 3/30 = 1/10. So, working together, the pumps will fill the pool in 10 hours."" This is the correct answer, but I was curious about how to know that the answer would be a whole number. Is there a way to look at the problem and know that the answer will be a whole number before doing any calculations? I appreciate your help.

You're right to be curious about why the answer will be a whole number before calculating. In this case, we can determine that the result will be a whole number by examining the fractions involved in the problem.

When we add 1/10 and 1/15, we're adding two fractions that have a denominator of 30 (the least common multiple of 10 and 15). The sum of these fractions is 3/30, which simplifies to 1/10. This means that when we express the combined rate as a fraction of the pool filled per hour, we get 1/10.

Now, when we set up the equation to find the time, we have 1/10 * time = 1 (the whole pool). Multiplying both sides by 10, we get time = 10 hours.

The reason we know this will result in a whole number is because when two rates are added (in this case, the rates of the two pumps), their combined rate will always be a fraction where the denominator is the least common multiple of the individual denominators. In this case, it's 30.

 Whenever you see fractions being added with a denominator that is the least common multiple (LCM) of the individual denominators, you can be confident that the sum will simplify to a fraction with a denominator of the LCM. This means that when you solve for the time, the result will always be a whole number of hours (assuming the work can be completed in a reasonable time)."
