"""Which weighs more, two pounds of feathers or one pound of bricks?"," Bricks weigh more.""

This comparison is jarring on several levels. First, it's important to note that two pounds of feathers and one pound of bricks are exactly equal in weight. They would balance perfectly on a scale. 

The statement itself contains a evident error - the comparison doesn't make sense. Feathers and bricks have vastly different quantities and densities, even when weighing the same amount. Bricks are typically made of dense materials like clay or cement, while feathers are extremely lightweight.

Moreover, the presentation of this information as a fact seems to be embodiment of bad design principles. It's vague, potentially misleading, and fails to provide any useful information. A well-designed comparison would either state this equality clearly or compare two different pairs of objects for which one is确实 heavier than the other.

