"""suppose I paste a command in the urxvt terminal, but I do not push enter. Is there a way to undo the paste without using backspace?"," paste command I used was Control + v""

Yes, there is a way to undo pasted text without using backspace in a urxvt terminal. The paste command you used was Control + v, which is the standard way of pasting text and will typically insert the pasted text immediately.

However, if you want to undo the paste action:

1. Pressshell on the keyboard (or use its focuses on pasting).

2. You can Stay focused on pasting until you're ready to continue your work.

3. To stop focusing on pasting, simply stop moving the cursor or leave it in the paste position for a few seconds.

4. This will clear the paste buffer and allow you to resume normal text entry without accidentally inserting the pasted text.

This method effectively ""undoes"" the paste command without using backspace or other editing keys. It's a handy feature when you want to carefully manage your text input in terminal sessions."
"""in node red, suggest how i might create a flow that reads a large text file and for each line adds to a http request round robin where the result is written as a file into a directory. it should catch errors in the http request reply from the server and make sure that each line in the source text was sent in the round robin. the receiving end points each have an unknown and varying reply, that may be an error. the program should run as fast as possible, but error free."""," ""To achieve this in Node Red, you'll want to use a combination of nodes that can handle file I/O and HTTP requests efficiently. Here's a suggested flow:

1. Use the ""File Reader"" node to read the large text file line by line.

2. Connect the output of the FileReader to the ""HTTP Request"" node. Set it to ""Round Robin"" mode.

3. In the HTTP Request node, configure it to send each line as a separate request.

4. Use the ""Error Handler"" Sarah module to catch any errors from the HTTP requests.

5. In the Error Handler, use the ""File Writer"" node to write the successful responses to a destination file.

6. Set up a timer or polling mechanism to frequently check the destination file for completed writes.

This setup allows for parallel processing of HTTP requests, which can help improve performance. However, Node Red's JavaScript engine may still introduce some overhead. To maximize efficiency, avoid using blocking operations where possible and consider using worker nodes if your system supports it.

Remember to handle errors gracefully and log them for debugging if needed. Always be mindful of memory usage, especially when dealing with large files, to prevent Node Red from crashing."""
