"""How does neural magic make models so fast"""," This neural magic technology enables Bird's-eye view navigation for mobile robots without LIDAR

Neural magic's technology accelerates deep learning models by several times. This speedup allows mobile robots equipped with cameras to perform bird's-eye view navigation without the need for expensive LIDAR systems.

The magic lies in their novel sparse inference technique that leverages the fact that real-world scenes are largely hollow. By recognizing the abundance of empty space in an image, the model can make smart assumptions about what's there and isn't.

This approach, which neural magic has developed over the last decade, dramatically reduces computational requirements. As a result, mobile robots can navigate their environments quickly and efficiently, using only lightweight cameras rather than costly LIDAR systems.

The efficiency of their technology is so significant that it's now possible to run complex reinforcement learning algorithms on embedded systems like the washboard Raspberry Pi. This opens up new possibilities for autonomous navigation in various outdoor and indoor settings."
"""Provide an introduction describing basic network\\narchitecture. Focus on the TCP\\/IP suite showing the\\nlayers, protocols and PDUs, and threats the network\\narchitecture"""," in no less than 2 paragraphs. Network architecture is the structural framework of a network, providing the foundation for communication and data transfer. The TCP/IP (Transmission Control Protocol/Internet Protocol) suite is an open standard protocol suite that forms the basis of the Internet and many private networks. It consists of several layers, each with specific responsibilities. The layers include the physical layer, which defines the physical characteristics of the network medium; the data link layer; the network layer; the transport layer; and the application layer. Protocols within the TCP/IP suite include network interface protocols like Ethernet, addressing protocols such as IP, and transport protocols like TCP and UDP. These protocols work together to Send,Receive and Guaranteed Transfers of Data (TCP) or Send,Receive>Data (UDP). Network threats can include malicious code, hacking attempts, denim, worms, denial of service attacks, and other cyber-Attacks that can compromise the integrity, availability, and Confidentiality of network resources and data."
