"""I want to test program in case of insufficient memory on Linux. How can I set a hard constraint on program's memory?"," I don't want to check Documentation above, just don't want hard Periods.""

This question seems to be looking for a built-in Linux kernel feature to limit a process's memory usage. The answer suggests using the `ulimit` command to set a memory limit. 

However, it's important to note that this will only work for the current user's shell session. For a system-wide limit or to apply it to all processes, additional steps would be needed.

The man page for `ulimit` provides more details and options for memory limits. It's typically located in `/etc/security/ulimit.conf` for default settings and can be adjusted as needed.

Remember, memory limits in Unix-like systems are not strict. Processes can still increase memory usage above the limit if the OS has extra memory available. So while this can be a useful tool for testing LeakyBuffers, it should be used with caution."
