"""Is seditious conspiracy a superset of insurrection?"," No, seditious conspiracy is not a superset of insurrection. 

While both seditious conspiracy and insurrection involve resistance to lawful authority, they have distinct elements and scopes. A seditious conspiracy is a secret agreement to overthrow the government or legal order, often focusing on planning and coordination. Insurrection, on the other hand, implies the actual use of force or
违法行动 to resist lawful authority. 

For example, a group plotting to assassinate key political figures would be a seditious conspiracy, but not necessarily an insurrection if they haven't actually carried out the violent actions. Vice versa, an actual violent uprising attempting to take power would be considered an insurrection, but might not be classified as a seditious conspiracy if it lacks premeditated planning.

Therefore, seditious conspiracy is a subset of insurrection, as it's a narrower and more specific type of rebellion that侧重于阴谋 and preparation, rather than the direct display of 400力量 that characterizes an insurrection."""
"""If in a price chart of a stock or index the price touches the lower bollinger band\\/ lower keltner channel one can never say with reasonable certainty that the price will stop falling further as it is already an oversold position as the price may keep falling further and the keltner channel or bollinger band will adjecu accordingly. Similarly, if the price touches the upper bands it may keep rising, the doesnt use of bollinger band or keltner channel defeats the purpose of identification of trend reversal ?"," What do you call this kind of a situation ?""

This situation is commonly referred to as ""overactivation"" or ""过度激活"" in technical analysis terms. It occurs when price breaks through a technical indicator's support or阻力 level that was previously identifying a trend direction. In this case, the breaking of the lower Bollinger Band or Keltner Channel suggests that the trend may be reversing or changing direction.

The issue with using such a strategy consistently is that it may lead to selling too soon or entering positions too late, potentially missing out on significant price movements. Technical analysts often prefer to use these bands more as range guides rather than definitive trend reversal signals to account for this overactivation effect.

A better approach might involve using these bands as support and resistance levels for trend continuation rather than trend reversals, and combining them with other indicators or Elliot wave analysis for a more comprehensive market view."
