"""An individual has an income of 10,000 after taxes, after all expense their net monthly income is 5,000. They have a line of credit  of 50,000 at 15% interest available for use, how can the individual increases their monthly net cash flow every year by 100,000?"," There are no constraints, no restrictions as to when the money is use, how much is used, from which account, just 100,000 increase in net cash flow every year."" The question is strange because you can just take all of the money and immediately increase your net cash flow by 100,000 a year.

The semi-formal�er solution though would be as follows:

1. Calculate annual interest: 50,000 * 0.15 = 7,500
2. Add interest to initial principal: 50,000 + 7,500 = 57,500
3. Repay 100,000 annually
4. Remaining balance: 57,500 - 100,000 = -42,500
   (You go into debt by 42,500)

5. Next year, calculate interest on new balance: 42,500 * 0.15 = 6,375
6. Add interest: 42,500 + 6,375 = 48,875
7. Repay 100,000
8. Remaining balance: 48,875 - 100,000 = -51,125
   (You go into deeper debt by 51,125)

The negative balances indicate that this At current interest rates, the individual cannot increase their monthly net cash flow by 100,000 annually while staying solvent.

To make this work, they would need a lower interest rate on their credit or additional income sources not accounted for in the initial question."
