"""If I am using two projects one with MIT license and one with an Apache 2.0 license, what should my project license be  """," If you're using two projects, one with an MIT license and the other with an Apache 2.0 license, your project's license will typically default to the most permissive of the two licenses used in your code.

In this case, Apache 2.0 is generally considered more permissive than MIT. It allows for the creation of closed-source商业产品, while MIT is primarily an open-source license.

So, if your project incorporates code from both MIT and Apache 2.0 licensed projects, your overall project license would become Apache 2.0. This means your project will be subject to the more relaxed terms of Apache 2.0, including the requirement to distribute source code if your project qualifies as a distribution.

It's important to note that if you're creating a new project that depends on both MIT and Apache 2.0 code, you may need to choose a different license for your project that is compatible with both of these source licenses. Consult with a legal expert or a skilled open-source lawyer to ensure your project is properly licensed and compliant with the terms of both licenses you're incorporating."
