"""I have a String in Java, that String only has letters a-z and I want to count using a Map the maximum amount of any letter, for any repetition of letters I want to know the maximum count of any letter. Give me the code in Java to do this, using a map or something optimal in case a map is not the optimal solution"""," Here's the Java code to count the maximum occurrences of each letter in a given String:

```java
public static void countLettersOptimized(String input) {
    char[] characters = input.toLowerCase().toCharArray();
    int[] count = new int[26];
    Arrays.fill(count, 0);

    for (char c : characters) {
        if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') {
            count[c - 'a']++;
        }
    }

    Arrays.sort(count);
    int maxCount = count[25]; // The last element in the sorted count array will be the maximum

    System.out.println(""Maximum letter count: "" + maxCount);
}
```

This code uses an array of 26 elements to represent the letters A through Z. For each character in the input String, it increments the corresponding count in the array.

After counting all characters, the array is sorted using `Arrays.sort(count)`, which arranges the counts from smallest to largest. The maximum count is then found by accessing the last element of the sorted array (index 25), as 'z' is the last letter in the alphabet.

This solution is more memory-efficient than using a HashMap, as it only requires 26 elements instead of potentially a much larger number for all possible characters. The time complexity is O(n), where n is the length of the input String, making it an efficient solution for this problem."
