To calculate the perimeter of a cube when given the length of an edge, we need to look at how the cube's faces relate to each other in 3D space.

A cube has 6 square faces arranged symmetrically. When we count the visible faces, we're essentially identifying how many edges are shown.

For any given edge of a cube, its opposite edge is directly parallel and at the same distance from the vertex. This means:

1. There are always 2 horizontal edges
2. There are always 2 vertical edges
3. There is 1 diagonal edge connecting them

So, if we count n visible faces, we have:
- 2n parallel edges (n horizontal, n vertical)
- 2n diagonal edges (n from each counts of horizontal and vertical)
- No other edges visible in this view

