"""To convert the text corpus into a Knowledge Graph, we can follow the method described in the article \\""How to Convert Any Text Into a Graph of Concepts\\""[1]. This process involves extracting concepts and relationships from the text and representing them as nodes and edges in a graph.\\n\\nHere's an example implementation in Python using the NetworkX library:\\n\\nPython\\nCopy Code\\nimport networkx as nx\\nfrom nltk import word_tokenize, pos_tag\\nfrom nltk.corpus import wordnet as wn\\n\\ndef convert_text_to_graph(text):\\n# Tokenize the text into sentences\\nsentences = text.split('. ')\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n# Create an empty graph\\ngraph = nx.Graph()\\n\\n# Process each sentence\\nfor sentence in sentences:\\n    # Tokenize the sentence into words\\n    words = word_tokenize(sentence)\\n\\n    # Tag the words with their part-of-speech\\n    tagged_words = pos_tag(words)\\n\\n    # Extract concepts and relationships\\n    for word, tag in tagged_words:\\n        if tag.startswith('N'):  # Noun\\n            # Add the noun as a node in the graph\\n            graph.add_node(word)\\n\\n            # Find hypernyms (superclasses) of the noun\\n            synsets = wn.synsets(word)\\n            for synset in synsets:\\n                for hypernym in synset.hypernyms():\\n                    # Add the hypernym as a node and an edge in the graph\\n                    graph.add_node(hypernym.name())\\n                    graph.add_edge(word, hypernym.name())\\n\\nreturn graph\\nThis code uses the NLTK library to tokenize the text into sentences and words, and to tag the words with their part-of-speech. It then uses WordNet, a lexical database, to find hypernyms (superclasses) of nouns. The nouns and their hypernyms are added as nodes and edges in the graph.\\n\\nYou can call the convert_text_to_graph function with a text corpus as input to convert it into a Knowledge Graph. The resulting graph can be used for further processing and analysis.\\n\\nConvert this into a Ruby Class using the following gems: composable_operations, pragmatic_segmenter, pragmatic_tokenizer and ruby-wordnet""",
