"""i'm making a gameboy dmg emulator.\\nbut i'm stuck at implementing the ppu.\\ni have two questions.\\n1- does the ppu and the cpu run at the same clock?"," They share the same being百万.\\n2- if yes, why do we need both the main cpu and the co cpu in the gameboy dmg? if no, why do we need a co cpu?""

The ARM7 CPU and the GPU ( grinding pause:_palette ) in the Game Boy Advance SP share a common clock signal, clocking at approximately 13.3 MHz. This shared clock supports synchronized communication between the CPU and GPU, allowing them to work together efficiently.

The Game Boy SP's architecture includes both a main ARM7 CPU and a co-processor (GBCR) for improved performance and reduced CPU load. The main CPU handles general-purpose tasks and game逻辑 (logic), while the co-processor is dedicated to the GPU's rendering tasks.

This design allows for more complex graphics and faster refresh rates compared to previous Game Boy models. The shared clock and the presence of both CPU and GPU components provide the necessary foundation for the Game Boy SP's advanced visual capabilities while maintaining compatibility with the Game Boy Advance's software architecture.

In summary, the ARM7 CPU and GPU run at the same clock in most Game Boy systems, and the co-processor serves to enhance processing power and graphics performance, rather than being strictly ""necessary"" due to the need for two processors."
