"""Write a lesson plan for a B2 English student who wants to get further practice the structure of 'inversions'."""," Here is a structured lesson plan for B2 English students to practice inversions:

Lesson Plan: Inversions for B2 English Students

1. Lesson objectives:
   - Students will understand the concept of inversion
   - They will practice identifying inversions in sentences
   - Students will learn to use inversion in their own writing

2. Material needed:
   - Whiteboard (if available)
   - Projector or screen for visual aids
   - sidesheet worksheet

3. Warm-up activity:
   • Quick oral survey: ""What fragile things do you usually keep in your pocket?""
   (Students provide answers, emphasizing words starting with 'in')


4. Introduction to inversions:
   • Define inversion: reversing the subject and auxiliary verb order
   • Examples:
     • Original: In his pocket he usually keeps his glasses.
     • Inverted: He usually keeps his glasses in his pocket.
   • Discuss the purpose of inversions and how they can create emphasis or form yes/no questions


5. practicing with sentences:
   • Provide a list of sentences with examples of inversions
   • split into small groups to discuss and practice
   • Role play exercises:
     • Student A: ""In her bag she carries tastes of troubled times.""
     • Student B: ""Tastes of troubled times she carries in her bag.""


6. Writing activity:
   • Students write paragraphs incorporating at least three inversions
   • Emphasize proper usage of inversion with common auxiliary verbs (e.g., do, does, did)


7. feedback and conclusion:
   • Students exchange and read each other's paragraphs
   • Teacher provides feedback on common errors and good practices
   • Recap key points about inversions and when to use them

8. Homework:
   • Students practice writing sentences with inversions in their daily lives
   • Create a short story using at least five inversions
