"""I want to run multiple container using docker compose, the image and settings are always the same, only thing is one environment variable is changing and the port. What would be a nice way to write the docker compose file ?",""" To efficiently add multiple containers with varying environment variables in a single Docker Compose file, you can use environment variables and service options effectively:

1. Define environment variables in your composition file:

```yaml
web:
  image: my_backend_app
  environment:
    ENV_VAR_1: value_1
    ENV_VAR_2: value_2

database:
  image: my_database_app
  environment:
    dental_service_mode: production
```

2. Use environment interpolate problems:

```yaml
web:
  environment:
    DJANGO_DEVELOPMENT_MODE: false
    DB_HOST: $DB_HOST
    DB_PORT: $DB_PORT
  volumes:
    - ""$DB_VOLUME:/ databases""
```

3. Use environment interpolation in environment variables and secrets file:

```
db_host=$(docker secrets get DB_HOST)
db_port=$(docker secrets get DB_PORT)

web:
  environment:
   [
      ""DJANGO_DEVELOPMENT_MODE"": false,
      ""DB_HOST"": ""${DB_HOST}"",
      ""DB_PORT"": ""${DB_PORT}"",
      ""DB_DATABASE"": ""$DB_DATABASE""
    ]
```
This approach allows you to define separate containers with background information in thewdx file, while dynamically populating environment variables and secrets to take advantage of the changing environment. It's a cleaner, more maintainable way to handle configuration for multiple containers within a single-compose file."
