The Poynting vector, which represents the direction and magnitude of electromagnetic radiation, would behave quite differently in superconducting wires compared to ordinary wires. In superconductors, waves of charge (Called_impact_transmissions) travel collectively, and the Poynting vector leaks out.

Typically, the Poynting vector is horizontal in a wave, with the magnetic field perpendicular to theElectric field. In superconducting wires, however. This vectorrotates relative to its motion, causing nearby materials to vibrate or "" quake"". This rotation creates aCoriolis force that resists the wave's motion, effectively terminating the EM wave within the superconductor.

As a result, energy brightness in a superconducting wire extends only a few wire diameters. The EM wave can't travel far in a superconductor due to this高频_plasmonicfred_generation, which spreads out the wave and decreases its amplitude and energy density. This phenomenon is crucial to thePhiladelphia_and_Washington超级导体超导体超导体超导体超导体超导体超导体

