""If f: A → B and g: B → C are both defined and their domains and codomains overlap, then it is possible to compose f and g. The composition f o g: A → C would be defined, where A is the domain of both f and g, and C is the codomain of the final composition.

However, if f and g are defined from the same domain A to the same codomain B (as mentioned in the original statement), then composition would not be possible because the elements of A would be mapped directly to B, bypassing the need for f and g at all.

