#!/usr/bin/env python3

import torch
import torch.nn as nn
import torch.nn.functional as F

class NeuralNet(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, num_units=10, dropout=0.1):
        super().__init__()
        self.num_units = num_units
        self.linear1 = nn.Linear(13, num_units)
        self.dropout = nn.Dropout(dropout)
        self.linear2 = nn.Linear(num_units, 10)
        self.linear3 = nn.Linear(10, 3)

    def forward(self, x):
        x = self.linear1(x)
        x = F.relu(x)
        x = self.linear2(x)
        x = F.relu(x)
        x = self.linear3(x)
        x = F.softmax(x, dim=1)
        return x

if __name__ == '__main__':
    neural_net = NeuralNet()
    print(torch.rand(1, 13))  # example input"
"""Please comprehend the question and choices first and then choose the right answer. Some of the questions may require multi-step reasoning. Explain your choice and your reasoning and explain why the rest of the options are not correct one be one.\\n\\nHarry met Bill, who was known to him to be a burglar, in a bar. Harry told Bill that he needed money. He promised to pay Bill $500 if Bill would go to Harry's house the following night and take some silverware. Harry explained to Bill that, although the silverware was legally his, his wife would object to his selling it. Harry pointed out his home, one of a group of similar tract houses. He drew a floor plan of the house that showed the location of the silverware. Harry said that his wife usually took several sleeping pills before retiring, and that he would make sure that she took them the next night. He promised to leave a window unlocked. Everything went according to the plan except that Bill, deceived by the similarity of the tract houses, went to the wrong house. He found a window unlocked, climbed in, and found silver where Harry had indicated. He took the silver to the cocktail lounge where the payoff was to take place. At that point police arrested the two men.\\""If Harry were charged with burglary, his best argument for acquittal would be that\\n\\nA: there was no breaking\\nB: he consented to the entry\\nC: no overt act was committed by him\\nD: there was no intent to commit a felony.""","""

In this situation, Harry's best argument for acquittal would be that he consented to the entry. This argument is based on the fact that Harry arranged for Bill to enter his home by leaving a window unlocked and providing a floor plan. He even0 explained the situation to Bill, telling him where to find the silverware. While this may not seem like explicit consent, it amounts to luring someone into the building with the intent to commit a crime. The other options are not as strong arguments for acquittal:

A: There was no breaking - Harry didn't forcible enter the building; Bill entered with his permission.
C: No overt act was committed by Harry - While he didn't physically enter, he primarily participated in the crime by facilitating it.
D: There was no intent to commit a felony - Harry was trying to arrange for the sale of stolen goods, which is still a felony, but the intent was much more limited than the actual crime Bill committed."
"""Write a simple example program that use canvas getImageData"""," A simple example program demonstrating how to use canvas getImageData in JavaScript:

