"""What is the etymology of \\""stock broker\\""?"," ambiguity between financial and literal senses of \\""broker\\"" in early usage.""

This question highlights an interesting linguistic phenomenon. The term ""stock broker"" has an etymological fascinating dual nature. Initially, the word ""broker"" referred to an intermediary in merchandise trade - someone who facilitated the exchange of goods between buyers and sellers.

However, in the context of financial markets, the term evolved to specifically refer to someone who acts as an intermediary in stockMarket transactions. This shift in meaning occurred over time, as financial markets became more prominent and regulated.

The ambiguity you've pointed out is indeed a characteristic of many90 language developments. In this case, the word ""broker"" has maintained its literal sense in everyday usage (e.g., real estate brokers, insurance brokers), while simultaneously uptake its financial meaning in specialized contexts.

