"""What is the critical path length of multiplying two square matrices of size NxN?"," What is it's time complexity? And what's the best case and worst case?""

The critical path length for multiplying two square matrices of size NxN is O(N^3), which corresponds to the cubic time complexity of the standard matrix multiplication algorithm.

This cubic complexity represents the best case, worst case, and typical performance of matrix multiplication. The O(N^3) bound applies to all these scenarios because the complexity is intrinsic to the algorithm and does not depend on specific input values.

It's worth noting that while the Strassen algorithm offers a complexity of O(N^2.81), the O(N^3) bound is still considered the bound for matrix multiplication. This is because Strassen's algorithm, although faster in theory, is not practical for most applications due to its additional complexity in terms of the number of operations and storage requirements."
