"""what is the fastest possible checkmate?"," 5 moves""

A5 to c5, Bb5+, Qd1, Rxc6, Kf1

This sequence completes a Rook fork on d4. The g-pawn, strongly caught5, has no way to defend against both the fork threat from d4 and the capture of c5 by the rook. The resulting dynamic positions two white pieces against two black pieces and one loose white piece, making it difficult to coordinate a meaningful defense.

This fork is often considered one of the most reliable mid-end games, though it's important to note that fork threats are generally more effective against solid openings than against openings with active pieces. In this case, the catch5 has resulted in a fork threat that is strong enough to cause mate in an advanced phase of the game."
