"""Write link list in c that is optimized for performance"""," To write a optimized performance link list in C, we need to use a doubly linked list data structure. This structure stores each element along with forward and backward pointers, allowing for efficient insertion, deletion, and traversal operations.

Here's a skeleton code for an optimized doubly linked list in C:

```c
typedef struct node {
    int data;
    struct node *prev;
    struct node *next;
} Node;

Node *create_node(int data) {
    Node *new_node =malloc(sizeof(Node));
    new_node->data = data;
    new_node->prev = NULL;
    new_node->next = NULL;
    return new_node;
}

void insert(Node **head, int data) {
    Node *new_node = create_node(data);
    if (*head == NULL) {
        *head = new_node;
        return;
    }
    Node *curr = *head;
    while (curr->next != NULL) {
        curr = curr->next;
    }
    curr->next = new_node;
    new_node->prev = curr;
}

void delete(Node **head, int data) {
    Node *curr = *head;
    Node *prev = NULL;
    while (curr != NULL) {
        if (curr->data == data) {
            if (prev != NULL) {
                if (curr->next != NULL) {
                    prev->next = curr->next;
                }
                else {
                    prev->next = NULL;
                }
                free(curr);
                return;
            }
        }
        else {
            prev = curr;
            curr = curr->next;
        }
    }
}

void display(Node *head) {
    Node *curr = *head;
    while (curr != NULL) {
        printf(""%d "", curr->data);
        curr = curr->next;
    }
}
```

This implementation memory-optimizes the structure by using a single pointer field per node, eliminating the need for separate pointers for previous and next nodes. This approach can slightly reduce memory usage at the cost of a minor decrease in performance for some operations.

The list mgmt functions shown above (insert, delete, and display) all have O(n) time complexity in the worst case, where n represents the number of nodes in the list. This makes the doubly linked list efficient for a wide range of applications, especially those requiring frequent insertions and deletions in a random order."
