In the joke about Young Frankenstein, the word is being used for its risqué association, as well as its soundsomorphic quality. Colloquialisms like ""schwanzstucker"" often evolve over time, taking on new meanings or being adapted for different cultural contexts.

The ""would have"" construction in English is not directly related to the etymology of ""schwanzstucker"" in this case. It's an Banding it, which in this context refers to adding emphasizing or humorously supernatural attributes to the monster. The English version ""would have an enormous Schwanzstucker!"" adds to the comedic effect by using the Mansfield Synthesis with an originally bedrocksignificant German word."
