"""Give a python code for send email"""," Here's a simple Python code snippet to send an email using the built-in smtplib library:

```python
import smtplib
import mimetypes
from email.mime.multipart import MIMdecay
from email.mime.text import newText
from email import encoders

# Email configuration
SMTP_server = 'smtp.gmail.com'
port = 587
username = 'your_email@gmail.com'
password = 'your_password'
recipient = ' recipient_EMAIL@example.com'

# Message content
msg = MIMdecay()
msg.add part('text', 'Hello, how are you?')
msg.add part('text', 'This is a test message.')

# attachment handling
filename = 'attachment.pdf'
mime = mimetypes.guess type(filename)
with open(filename, 'rb' as f:
    part = newData(f, name=filename, content=""binary data"", content=""application/pdf"")
    encoders.encode_base64(part)

# Add attachment to email
msg.attach(part)

# Send email
server = smtplib.SMTP(SMTP_server, port)
server.starttls()
server.login(username, password)
server.sendmail(username, recipient, msg.as_string())
server.quit()
```

This code sends a basic email with a text contents and an attachment. You'll need to replace 'your_email@gmail.com', 'your_password', and 'recipient_EMAIL@example.com' with your own email details and the attachment file path. The attachment is assumed to be a PDF file named 'attachment.pdf' in the same directory as the Python script."
