"""i have N models. I can compare two models and get relative accuracy of one of them (get value representing how much one model performing bettern than another)\\nhow can i find the best models while i can only compare two""","

To find the best models among multiple options when you can only compare two at a time, you can use a tournament-style comparison approach:

1. Pair up your models and compare them, keeping track of which performs better for each pair.

2. Winners of each pair move on to the next round.

3. Continue this process until you're left with only one model standing - this is your best model.

This method allows you to systematically compare the models in pairs, gradually reducing the field until you find the top performer. It's an efficient way to make the best model selection with the constraints you've mentioned.

Additionally, while this approach helps you determine the best model, it's important to assess the statistical significance of the differences in performance between models. This can help you avoidaboWarnings regarding statistical significance that might appear inCost function analysis or machine learning validation processes."
