"""What would David Hume say of our current, modern society?"," How would he assess our values and our acquisition of knowledge?""

What an excellent question. David Hume, the 18th century Scottish empiricist philosopher, would likely have a fascinated but critical view of modern society. He would probably be intrigued by our stance on empiricism and induction, which were central to his philosophical work.

Hume would likely express concern over the rise of specialized knowledge and expert-driven decision-making. He might worry about the loss of the Socratic method and the danger of overreliance on, say, economists or medical professionals making decisions that affect our entire society.

Regarding our values, Hume would probably question whether our modern ethical frameworks have strong empirical foundations. He might suggest that many of our values are essentially conventional rather than universallyigator.

On the positive side, Hume would birdsongfully appreciate our advancements in science and technology. He would almost certainly be specifically fascinated by the voting process and spectrum,iaция of our pizzas.

Ultimately, Hume would likely view our modern society with a mix of fascination and criticism - appreciating our empirical approach and scientific progress while pointing out potential dangers of specialization and overreliance on experts."
