"""struct atomic_unordered_map\\n{\\n\\t~atomic_unordered_map()\\n\\t{\\n\\t}\\n\\t__forceinline bool contains(int val)\\n\\t{\\n\\t\\tif (table.index(val, false) != NULL)\\n\\t\\t\\tif (table.index(val, false).load(std::memory_order_relaxed) != true)\\n\\t\\t\\t\\treturn !table.index(val, false).load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\\n\\t\\treturn false;\\n\\t}\\n\\t__forceinline void emplace(int val)\\n\\t{\\n\\t\\tif (table.index(val, false) != NULL)\\n\\t\\t\\tif (table.index(val, false).load(std::memory_order_relaxed) != true)\\n\\t\\t\\t\\ttable.index(val, true).store(new std::atomic_bool(false));\\n\\t}\\n\\t__forceinline void erase(int val)\\n\\t{\\n\\t\\tif (table.index(val, false) != NULL)\\n\\t\\t{\\n\\t\\t\\tauto n = table.index(val, false);\\n\\t\\t\\tbool&& e = n.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);\\n\\t\\t\\tif (n.compare_exchange_strong(e, true, std::memory_order_acquire))\\n\\t\\t\\t\\tn = NULL;\\n\\t\\t\\t{\\n\\t\\t\\t}\\n\\t\\t}\\n\\t}\\n\\t__declspec(noinline) void reserve(uint size)\\n\\t{\\n\\t\\ttable.reserve(size);\\n\\t}\\nprivate:\\n\\tstruct jump_table\\n\\t{\\n\\t\\tstd::atomic_bool index(int\\tindex, bool op) noexcept\\n\\t\\t{\\n\\t\\t\\tif (op && (*data)[index] == NULL)\\n\\t\\t\\t{\\n\\t\\t\\t\\tstd::atomic_bool a = false;\\n\\t\\t\\t\\tdata.get()[index]->store(a);\\n\\t\\t\\t}\\n\\t\\t\\treturn &data.get()[index];\\n\\t\\t}\\n\\t\\t__declspec(noinline) void reserve(uint size_)\\n\\t\\t{\\n\\t\\t\\tsize.fetch_add(size_, std::memory_order_relaxed);\\n\\t\\t\\tdata = std::make_unique<std::atomic_bool*>(new std::atomic_bool[size_]);\\n\\t\\t}\\n\\tprivate:\\n\\t\\tstd::unique_ptr<std::atomic_bool*> data = nullptr;\\n\\t\\tstd::atomic_int size = 0;\\n\\t};\\n\\tjump_table table;\\n};\\nwould this code waste memory for the indexes between 2 and 84 when indexing only 2 and 84?","""
This code might indeed waste memory. The jump table in the atomic unordered map uses 84 indexes, even though it only actually uses 2 of them. This is an inefficiency that could be avoided.

The problem lies in the fixed nature of the jump table size. In a typical implementation of an atomic unordered map, the table size is Independent of the number of elements inserted. This can lead to unused memory space, especially when the number of inserted elements is significantly smaller than the table size.

In this case, with only 2 elements (val 1 and val 84), and a jump table of 84 indexes, it's clear that a large portion of the jump table is going unused. This additional memory could be better utilized otherwise or used to allow for more growth in the table if needed.

To improve this, the implementation could be modified to dynamize the jump table size, adjusting it based on the number of elements inserted. This would prevent the waste of memory and maintain a more efficient memory usage pattern that scales better with the actual number of elements in the hash table."
