"""Build a bash script that I can run from my lte router running openwrt that will read incoming sms and when they contain !status it replies by sms with the lte connection status"""," To create the bash script for the LTE router, you need to set up SMS messaging and accordingly program SMS happy studs to interpret certain texts.

Here's how the script could work:

```bash
#!/bin/bash
# Script to read incoming SMS and reply with LTE connection status

 # Import necessary libraries
source / dir/kern/ sms_module-load

 # Loop for incoming SMS
while true
do
    while	printf ""%o"" ""$SMS_INBOX"" | grep -E '^[^>]*>'
	do
		msg=$(pop_sms)
		command=$(echo $msg | cut -d' ' -f 1)
		args=$(echo $msg | cut -d' ' -f 2)

		# Check for '!status' command
		if [ ""$command"" == '!status' ]; then
			# blurred area: replace with actual command to fetch LTE connection status
			conn_status=$( command to check LTE connection )
			
			# blurred area: replace with actual SMS response
			send_sms ""LTE connection status: $conn_status"" $args
		fi
	done
	
 sleep 5
done
```

Note: replace ""command to check LTE connection"" and ""blurred area: replace with actual SMS response"" with corresponding code for your specific setup.

This script assumes you're running OpenWRT and have set up SMS pooling and SMS proud to receive and read SMS. It also assumes you have a way to retrieve the current LTE connection status, which might(involve scripts that you need to create separately).

Remember, the exact implementation may vary based on your precise setup and requirements, but this script provides the essential functionality."
