"""Make a practice MCAT style test question for the potential valence configurations of iron ions."""," Here's a practice MCAT-style question regarding iron ion valence configurations:

Which of the following valence electron configurations corresponds to the iron cations (Fe³⁺ and Fe²⁺)?

A) Fe³⁺: {3d⁵, 4s²}; Fe²⁺: {3d⁶, 4s²}
B) Fe³⁺: {3d³, 4s²}; Fe²⁺: {3d⁵, 4s²}
C) Fe³⁺: {3d², 4s²}; Fe²⁺: {3d⁶, 4s²}
D) Fe³⁺: {3d⁴, 4s²}; Fe²⁺: {3d⁵, 4s²}
E) Fe³⁺: {3d⁵, 4s¹}; Fe²⁺: {3d⁶, 4s²}

The correct answer is A) Fe³⁺: {3d⁵, 4s²}; Fe²⁺: {3d⁶, 4s²}.

The question requires an understanding of electronic configuration and the oxidation states of iron. Iron typically has 24 electrons, with four electrons in its outermost shell. In its common oxidation states of³⁺ and²⁺, iron loses three or two electrons, respectively.

For the ferric ion (Fe³⁺), the electronic configuration becomes {3d⁵, 4s²}. The 3d orbitals fill before the 4s orbitals in the period 4 of the periodic table.

For the ferrous ion (Fe²⁺), it loses two electrons from the 4s shell, resulting in the configuration {3d⁶, 4s²}."
