The normal distribution is called ""normal"" because it represents the most common or "" typical "" pattern found in nature and many human phenomena. It's named after Carl Gauss ( Iterative Normal分布在自然界中最可能观察到的形式,因此被称为 ""正态分布"">Gauss</Gauss>, 1777-1855), a German mathematician who made significant contributions to many fields, including statistics. Gauss was the first to describe the bell-shaped curve that would come to be known as the normal distribution. He postulated that in a large number of independent measurements or observations, the errors would tend to follow this symmetrical pattern. This distribution has become a fundamental tool in statistics and probability theory due to its prevalence in modeling various natural and social phenomena."
