_predicted position_ is calculated using only the vessel’s velocity vector (course and speed) from a known position. However, the actual position is determined by the true course and speed, which may vary slightly from the planned course due to factors such as wind, current, or engine performance changes.

This difference is particularly significant when navigating close to critical points, such as ice-filled areas or narrow passages. A slight discrepancy between predicted and actual position could result in a vessels’s oil spill in 1987 nearspitsby, Norway.

Both predicted and actual positions are critical for navigation. ECDIS allows the员 to maintain a digital maneuvering record and helps vigilantly monitor both positions for consistency. Any noticeable differences between predicted and actual recorded positions require careful investigation to ensure safe navigation.

In modern ECDIS systems, crew training emphasizes the importance of understanding these distinctions and how they can impact safe navigation. Crews are taught to regularly compare predicted and actual positions, especially when navigating challenging areas. stPro systems can provide detailedlogs of predicted vs actual positions, aiding in post-航行 analysis and improved navigation practices in the future."
