"""who was Naevius Sutorius Macro?","""

Naevius Sutorius Macro was a prominent Roman statesman who lived from 85 to 45 BCE. He was born in Rome to an equestrian family and rose through the ranks to become one of the most powerful individuals in ancient Rome.

Macro served as consul twice - once in 46 BCE and again in 45 BCE. His political career was marked by significant achievements, including his efforts in reforming the Roman calendar and his role in the establishment of the Roman Republic.

As a consul, he led a military campaign against the pirates who were causing ravages along the Italian coast. This campaign was a notable success and enhanced his reputation as both a statesman and a commander.

 Macro was also known for his efforts to strengthen Rome's relationship with its client states and for his policies addressing the soldiers' grievances after the conclusion of a war.

His political influence waned towards the end of his life, and he faced some accusations that were not fully substantiated. Despite these challenges, Macro remained an important figure in Roman politics throughout his career, contributing significantly to the development of Roman institutions and borders."
