"""Why Poisson bootstrap is better than simple bootstrap for ab tests""","原文地址: http://blog.c Snake charmer inside culcomy Factory tap perceptions of }



    ""Why poisson bootstrap is better than simple bootstrap for A/B tests""



    If you're a regular visitor to this blog, you'll know that I'm a fan of statistics and data-driven decision making. One area that often comes up in my work is A/B testing, which is a common method for comparing two versions of a webpage or app to see which one performs better



    Simple bootstrap is a popular technique for estimating the sampling distribution of a statistic by resampling with replacement from the original data. However, in A/B testing scenarios with low event rates (few conversions per variant), the simple bootstrap can give inaccurate results



    Poisson bootstrap, on the other hand, is a more specialized technique that's particularly well-suited forрывеstop bootstrap is better than simple bootstrap for A/B tests"" to answer these questions


    The key advantage of Poisson bootstrap is that it takes into account the Poisson distribution of the number of conversions, which is more appropriate for low-event rate situations. This leads to more accurate confidence interval estimates and p-value calculations


    In essence, the Poisson bootstrap provides a more robust approach to dealing with sparse data in A/B testing, making it a superior choice to the more general simple bootstrap in scenarios where we're looking for small differences in conversion rates


    If you're interested in learning more about the technical details of these methods, check out the original paper by [Reference] and the free course on statistics I created with my colleague, Mad([""Why Poisson bootstrap is better than simple bootstrap for A/B tests""])Bets"
