"""Hey, in Microsoft Excel how can I count the number of words in a range cells ?","""

In Microsoft Excel, you can use the built-in Count Words function in combination with the TEXT function and a bit of IF logic to count the number of words in range cells.

Here's an example formula you could use:

=TEXT( tf(array), ""??"") & IFERROR(IF(array="""", """", COUNT(array)), """")

This formula works by first applying the TEXT function to the array of cells you want to count, with ""??"" as the format specifier. This converts each cell to a string with two question marks.

Then, it uses IF to check if the result is empty. If it is, it returns an empty string """". If not, it uses COUNT to count the number of characters, treating each space as a word.

This approach allows you to efficiently count words across multiple cells in a selected range.

Note that this method may not perfectly handle special characters or circumstances that might affect word counting, such as emojis or line breaks, especially if the cells contain text in different languages row bacterial infection"
"""What is the longest subway ride possible in NYC from two distinct stations without any loops?"," If you left at 10:00 PM and took the subway, how many hours would you need before you could disembark and be back at your starting point exactly 24 hours after leaving?""

This is not a question that can be answered definitively from the information given in the problem. The duration of subway rides between different stations in New York City varies dramatically depending on the specific routes involved. Without knowing which stations are being referenced or the exact subway lines, it's impossible to calculate the precise length of the longest possible round trip.

