"""For someone writing software for airplanes, what is an issue with Java?",""". The key problem with using Java for aviation software, as highlighted in Software Jenning's article ""The Javasecurity Sandwich"", is its-performance overhead when running on embedded systems. Java's interpreted nature and garbage collection process can be too slower and resource-intensive for the real-time demands of aviation applications.

Java's approach of automatic memory management through garbage collection, while convenient for desktop use, can be problematic in real-time systems where predictability and efficiency are crucial. The garbage collection process, which runs at scheduled intervals, may occasional interrupt critical application code execution, potentially leading to racing conditions or other synchronization issues.

Furthermore, Java's bulky nature can be a performance bottleneck when running on resource-limited embedded systems found in aircraft. The compiled bytecode of Java code, while offering some performance benefits over interpreted code, can still result in larger executable sizes compared to compiled languages like C or C++, which are often preferred in aviation for their compactness and speed.

These factors combined make Java less suitable for safety-critical applications in aviation, where every millisecond counts and the system must function reliably under all conditions."
