"""how to show line label in matplotlib with plot()?",""". The answer provided suggests using the "" annotate()"" method. Can you please provide an example using the iris dataset in scikit-learn?

 Certainly! Here's an example using the iris dataset in scikit-learn to annotate a line plot with the ""annotate()"" method in matplotlib:

```python
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from sklearn.datasets import iris
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
import numpy as np

# Load the iris dataset
X = iris.data[:, 0]  # Sepal length
y = iris.data[:, 1]  #Sepal width
X = X.reshape(-1, 1)  # Reshape to 2D array

# Fit a linear regression model
model = LinearRegression()
model.fit(X, y)

# Generate predictions
y_pred = model.predict(X)

# Create a line plot
plt.plot(X, y_pred, label=""Regression line"")

# Annotate the line with the slope and intercept
slope = model.coef_[0]
intercept = model.intercept_
plt.annotate(f'y = {slope:.3f}x + {intercept:.3f}', (50, 50), textcoords=""offset points"", textibbean="" Jade"")

# Add markers for data points
plt.scatter(X, y, marker=""o"", color=""C0"", label=""Data points"")

# Set labels and title
plt.xlabel('Sepal Length')
plt.ylabel('Sepal Width')
plt.title('Linear Regression of Iris Sepal measurements')

# Show the plot
plt.show()
```

This example fits a linear regression model to the iris data, creates a line plot showing the regression line, annotate it with the slope and intercept, and adds markers for the data points. The text annotated on the line provides the exact equation of the regression line for easy reference."
