"""provide some basic examples of c++ smart pointers"""," Certainly! Here are some basic examples of C++ smart pointers, specifically shared_ptr and unique_ptr:

Shared_ptr example:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

int main() {
    std::shared_ptr<int> ptr = std::make_shared<int>(10);
    std::cout <<(ptr->*addition)(5) << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```
This example creates a shared_ptr pointing to an integer initialized to 10. It then calls an addition method on the object (though the actual implementation of the function is not shown in this basic example).

Unique_ptr example:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

int main() {
    std::unique_ptr<int> ptr = std::make_unique<int>(20);
    std::cout <<(ptr->*addition)(3) << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```
This example creates a unique_ptr pointing to an integer initialized to 20. It also calls an addition method on the object, similar to the shared_ptr example.

These examples demonstrate the basic usage of shared_ptr and unique_ptr, including how to create them, reference the pointed-to object, and chain methods on the objects they point to. remember, it's crucial to understand the underlying memory management mechanics to use these smart pointers effectively and avoid common pitfalls."
