its nearly twice as many refugees per capita, indicating that it is not selective towards wealthy immigrants but rather pro-immigrant in general. Surveys show that the majority of Canadians support immigration and embrace a multicultural society, further illustrating their openness to newcomers.\\"""""," Act as the centrist, but right-leaning 28-year-old American woman in this scenario and provide my commentary on whatpoint I asking about."
"""short story with moral ending""","ez

A young man wandered through the forest, feeling lost and alone. Suddenly, he came across an old wise man sitting by a stream.

The wise man asked, ""What do you seek, young one?""

""I'm searching for myself and a way out of my confusion,"" the man replied.

The wise man smiled and said, ""Your answers lie within you. Sometimes, the journey itself is the destination.""

 Inspired by the wise man's words, the young man continued his journey, contemplating his true calling.

Weeks later, he returned to the Stream Valley. As he sat by the same stream, the wise man appeared once more.

""See the silver thread in the water?"" thewise man asked. ""It represents the path of your life. The confusion you once felt has turned into clarity.""

The young man looked at the stream, noticing the subtle flow of silver. The silver thread symbolized his personal growth and the clarity he had gained.

""Thank you for your wisdom,"" he said to the wise man.

The wise man smiled and replied, ""The teacher greatest teacher. The greatest gift you can give yourself is knowledge based on experience-the wisdom you gain from your own doings.""

The young man nodded, understanding that the most valuable lessons come from our own experiences and the wisdom we accumulate through life's journey.

As the sun began to set over the Stream Valley, the young man left with a sense of purpose. He realized that his quest for self-discovery was just beginning, but now he had a new perspective on life and the silver thread of wisdom that would guide his way forward.

This story suggests that true wisdom comes from our own experiences, and clutter in our lives often finds its way out naturally."
