"""In Iso C what is the behavior if I declare the main function as int but don't return a value """," and if I declare the main function with void but do return a value?

The behavior of declareing the main function as int but not returning a value is undefined. While the main function in C++ is typically required to return 0, C allows for some flexibility in defining functions with return types. However, if you don't return any value from a function with an int type signature (int main()), the program's behavior become undefined and may not behave as expected.

On the other hand, declarng the main function with void and still returning a value would result in a compile-time error. The void keyword indicates that the function doesn't return any value, so attempting to return a value of any type (including int) would violate this type signature.

C++ compiled by modern compilers would reject both of these scenarios. Modern C++ programs areV flam做好 to adhere to well-defined type signatures and return behaviors for better code safety and predictability. In aBon situation, awell HOLAccordingly C++ program should always defining main with int and return 0, if any value is to be returned from the main function."
