"""The fastest chess chekmate possible"""," --cmpr

In recent years, checking AI engines and engines solving Endgame tablebases have become more common. What would be the appeal of attempting to create an AI engine focused on making the fastest possible chess checkmate?

I am particularly interested in playing against such an engine. At first glance, developing this engine might seem quite simple - focus on tablebases and short mate possibilities. However, limits become quite quickly patches Located deeper in. An engine which Traveling to every depth of 20 humans games, builds tablebases from analyzing this Search and launch. Android Security

These engines can be quite fast, often reaching mate-like mate in 15 moves quickly. On popular online chess platforms like chess.com, engines with powerful graphics cards can easily calculate mates in 5-10 moves, sometimes even faster than human players. These engines often integrated into online chess games, allowing humans to play against them for a convenience.

So, what would a chess engine focused solely on making the fastest possible checkmates look like? How would it play? What tactical ideas would it prioritize? And how does this comparison change if we consider engines that prioritize longer mate lines, such as LK9, which appears to have a very munch endgame?

This type of engine development could create a fascinating arms race in chess AI, emphasizing extremely quick calculations over the full range of chess games. It might lead to some interesting tactical discoveries and possibly challenge our understanding of chess complexity."
