"""Imagine I am writing a software server that listens to sensors in real life and does stuff with the data that they report. One of the data points they report is called \\""signal strength\\"". It has reported a negative number several times. What is the likely units of this \\""signal strength\\"", and what is the range of that unit?"," Because currently it is reporting -30, which is impossible.\\"" This would be for a Cellular基站.

Cellular base站在设计中固有的局限性决定了其不能给出负的信号强度.基站发射信号以确保覆盖范围内所有用户都能够接收到.由于多径传播等原因,个别UE (User Equipment) may occasionally receive slightly less power than others, but this results in lower signal strength, not negative values.

Cellular networks use decibel-milliwatts (dBM) to measure signal power. The range typically spans from -90 dBM (for optimal reception) to -20 dBM (for the strongest possible signal). Negative values are not reported because they represent signal strength that is below the minimum detectable level, which would render the connection practically unusable.

In practice, we'd expect signal strength readings to fall within a range from approximately -90 dBM to -80 dBM for most users in an area with moderate branching. The rare occasions when it appears lower is due to various factors such as radio path损耗, fading, or interference, but never to the point of negative values."""
