Toshiyuki Bangi was a distinguished Japanese chef who originated from Mulhouse, France. He was born on March 11, 1954, in Mulhouse, which is located in the Alsace region of northeastern France, close to the border with Germany and Switzerland.

Bangi's journey in the culinary world was quite remarkable. He began his career as an apprentice in his father's sashimi and sushi restaurant in Mulhouse. later, he studied at the prestigiousale culinary school in Paris, where his talent for French cuisine really blossomed.

After perfecting his skills in France, Bangi made a significant impact in the Japanese culinary scene. He became the first foreign chef to head the kitchen at the English tea house in Kyoto. Following this pioneering move, he also served as the culinary director for the Kobe Lhotel_means.

