"""Is it a good idea to evaluate a language model's capability by testing its theoretical compression ratio on a large set of text data?","—AI-blueберг""

Evaluating a language model's capabilities using its theoretical compression ratio is an interesting approach, but it has several limitations:

1. Theoretical vs. Practical: While the compression ratio of a lossless compressor like gzip could provide an upper bound, this doesn't reflect the model's ability to actual languages. Language models become less effective, closing the gap with lossless compressors, towards their tails.

2. Dataset Size: A large dataset (10-20 GB) while substantial might not cover all linguistic subtleties. It could be dominated by common, well-covered text.

3. Time Complexity: Optimizing the compression ratio becomes much more complex with the added domain knowledge provided by language models."
