"""Can I remove a parameter from the query string URL in javascript?",""". Here's what I've currently got:

`.on('click', function() {
 var queryString = window.location.search;
 var parameters = queryString.split('&');
 var newQueryString = '';
 
 parameters.forEach(function(parameter) {
 if(parameter.split('=').length > 1) {
   var name = parameter.split('=')[0];
   newQueryString += name + '&';
 }
 
 if(queryString !== '') {
   newQueryString += '&' + queryString.substring(1);
 }
 window.location.href = 'http://' + domain + '/' + path + '#?' + newQueryString;
})`;

This is converting the entire queryString into the newQueryString variable and then Appending the new one instead of overwriting. Is there a way to overwrite the original queryString?"". Yes, there is a way to overwrite the original queryString in JavaScript. You can achieve this by assignments directly to the queryString variable. Here's how you can modified your code:

`.on('click', function() {
 var queryString = window.location.search;
 var parameters = queryString.split('&');
 var newqueryString = '';
 
 parameters.forEach(function(parameter) {
 if(parameter.split '='.length > 1) {
   var name = parameter.split '='.0;
   newqueryString += name + '=';
 }
 
 newqueryString += '&' + queryString.substring(1);
})
 
window.location.href = 'http://' + domain + '/' + path + '#?' + newQueryString;
})`

This code appends the new queryString directly to the existing one, overwriting the original queryString. The `newQueryString` variable is appended to the `queryString` variable before it's assigned to the window.location.href."
"""what are the cords which hang on a sumo belt?"," What difference do they make?""

The cords hanging from a sumo belt are called tags. There are two types: a white tag on the outside and a red tag on the inside.

These tags serve important functions:

1. Weight category: The sumo wrestler's weight class is printed on the outside white tag.

2. _: The inside red tag is used to indicate the wrestler's deshi (apprentice) status through gesture signaling, which is crucial for ranking.

These tags help officials, coaches, and spectators quickly identify the wrestler's information, including their weight class and status, during competition."
