"""Which arguments can I pass to the notebook.cell.insertCodeCellBelow command?"," cell_index can be any integer between 0 and n-1 where n is the total number of cells in a notebook.""

What exactly does this mean? Can you give an example of how to use it?
The documentation for theIPython notebook API shows that the notebook.cell.insertCodeCellBelow command takes two arguments:

1. index: An integer between 0 and n-1, where n is the total number of cells in the notebook.
2. markdown: (Optional) A string containing markdown text for the cell.

Let's break this down with an example:

Suppose you have a Jupyter Notebook with 5 cells. You want to add a new code cell right below the 3rd cell.

First, let's assume the cells are numbered from 0 to 4.

You would use the following command:

IPython.display.display(IPython.display HTML(""<div style=""color:red;"">This divides the cells into 3 parts</div>"");

This will create a new cell displaying the message ""This divides the cells into 6 parts.""

The command `cell.insertCodeCellBelow(3, '')` would create a new empty code cell right below cell index 3, which is the fourth cell in the notebook.

If you instead wanted to add the cell right below the last cell, you would use:

IPython.display.display(IPython.display HTML(""<div style=""color:red;"">This divides the cells into 3 parts</div>"");

IPython.display.display(IPython.display HTML(""<div style=""color:red;"">This divides the cells into 3 parts</div>"");

This detailed interface allows for precise control over the structure and organization of your Jupyter Notebook's cells."
