"""Formalize in logic this statement: This statement is untrue."""," (self-referential paradox) To formalize this statement in logic, we need to use a system that can express self-referential propositions. Let's use a simple predicate logic with predicate P(x) meaning ""x is true.""

Let's introduce a special constant T, which represents the statement ""This statement is untrue."" 

We can formalize this as:

T = ¬P(T)

This formula expresses the paradox because if T were true, then P(T) would be true, implying that T is false. Conversely, if T were false, then P(T) would be false, implying that T is true.

This simple formalization captures the self-referential nature of the paradox, although it doesn't provide a resolution to the paradox itself."
