"""what are acidic amino acids"""," The acidic amino acids in proteins are:

1. Aspartic acid (Asp)
2. Glutamic acid (Glu)
3. Asparagine (Asn)
4. cysteine (Cys)
5. serine (Ser)
6. threonine (Thr)
7. tyrosine (Tyr)

The first three - Aspartic acid, Glutamic acid, and Asparagine - are considered ""L-alpha amino acids"" because their side chains are L-hom=head amino acids. They are the most common acidic amino acids in proteins.

The remaining four - cysteine, serine, threonine, and tyrosine - are S-alpha and D-alpha amino acids, depending on the context.

These amino acids are crucial for protein structure and function, often forming important bonds and contributing to the overall folding and activity of proteins."
