"""Write a story about a conversation between a teenaged girl and her heavily pregnant middle aged mother"""," It was a typical Wednesday evening when I sat down with my mom at the kitchen table, my backpack slung over my shoulder and a stack of homework in front of me. The tension in the air was palpable, not something I'd grown accustomed to over the years.

""Sarah,"" Mom's voice cut through my thoughts, softer than usual. ""We need to talk about something.""

I looked up, my pen hovering over the math problem. ""What is it?""

Mom took a deep breath, her eyes pleading with me. ""Remember that guy from your class, Jake? Well, I... I'm pregnant by him.""

I felt my heart skip a beat. The room seemed to darken, and I could barely move. “Pregnant? By who? Why didn't you tell me?”

“Yes, by Jake. And I'm keeping it, Sarah.""

That was it. The rug was pulled out from under me in one fell swoop. All my carefully crafted plans for college and my perfectly scheduled summer job crumbled around my feet.

“We need to figure out what we're going to do,” Mom continued, her voice barely above a whisper.

“We? This is happening to you. It’s not my problem.” I felt angry, hurt, and more confused than I had in a long time.

“No, it isn’t. But this isn't just about me anymore. This baby, it needs a family, or a support system at least.”

I slumped back in my chair, clutching my textbook. ""And what about me? What about my dreams? My future?""

“We can work with that. We can make this work.”

“But how? We can't just... we can't just live together like some... like some generic TV show, Mom. There are complications involved.""

“I know, sweetie. I know. But we have to face this together, okay?""

Together. The word hit me like a punch to the gut. We had never been together like this before. No matter what we had been through as mother and daughter, nothing had prepared me for this moment.

“I don’t want people at school knowing,” I said, short of breath.

“I can handle that. We just have to be careful and strategic about everything.”

“They won’t believe how it’s happening. How can you be Pregnant? stop says Allie.”

“I know, I know. But I am, and here we are. We have a baby to think about now.”

In that moment, I realized that our relationship would never be the same. So much of what I had expected for my life was changing, and I wasn’t sure I was ready for it. But as I looked at my mom, despite my maneuverings and my frustration, I wanted to trust her. This was my mother, after all. Even when she made mistakes – and she always seemed to – she was there for me.

“We dirty dancing to figure this out,” I said, tslib gaining my humor back. “And we Slade- it all out together.”

Mom smiled, the corners of her eyes crinkling with real warmth for the first time 

“I think that sounds perfect, baby.”

