"""Describe chebyshev polynomials"""," The Chebyshev polynomials are a sequence of orthogonal polynomials defined on the interval [-1, 1]. There are two main types: T_n(x) and U_n(x), which can be generated using the recursive formulas:

T_0(x) = 1, T_1(x) = x
U_0(x) = 1, U_1(x) = 2x

For n ≥ 1, the polynomials satisfy the recurrence relations:

T_{n+1}(x) = 2xT_n(x) - T_{n-1}(x)
U_{n+1}(x) = 2xU_n(x) - U_{n-1}(x)

The first few Chebyshev polynomials are:

T_0(x) = 1
T_1(x) = x
T_2(x) = 2x^2 - 1
T_3(x) = 4x^3 - 3x
T_4(x) = 8x^4 - 8x^2 + 1
T_5(x) = 16x^5 - 20x^3 + 5x

U_0(x) = 1
U_1(x) = 2x
U_2(x) = 4x^2 - 1
U_3(x) = 8x^3 - 4x
U_4(x) = 16x^4 - 12x^2 + 1
U_5(x) = 32x^5 - 20x^3 - 5x

These polynomials have many applications in approximation Theory, numerical analysis, and signal processing due to their orthogonal properties and useful extremal characteristics."
